
On the Kind of Practitioner I Am
My practice and research center on photography. My work typically begins from the technical details of a medium's history, a camera technique, or a specific function, and then translates these into spatial works that can be experienced by the body: reconstructing a photographic theory or phenomenon into a spatial experience that the body can enter, perceive, and interact with.

Research Theme
The subjectivity of photography does not reside in the instant the shutter is pressed; rather, it is distributed across the process of "which parameters are ceded to the algorithm beforehand" and "which result is selected and how it is modified afterward." Accordingly, the classical theory of photographic subjectivity anchored in the "decisive moment" (the Cartier-Bresson—Barthes lineage) must, under the conditions of computational photography, be rewritten by a model of "distributed judgment."
In this research, the concept of the shashin kōi (the photographic act, 写真行為) is no longer bounded by a single point in time, but instead spans the entire temporal process of shooting. The study further asks: within the technical field opened up by computational photography (around 2016), when the shashin kōi is situated along the continuous spectrum of optical—computational—generative, how should it be rewritten under contemporary theoretical frameworks?

Premises and Theoretical Background
In recent years, photography has been generally defined as a medium that captures images through a camera and fixes and reproduces its subject. Yet certain theorists moved beyond "the image itself," turning instead toward "the act that produces the image."
Several thinkers have advanced concepts concerning the photographic act. In The Metaphor of the Eye (眼の隠喩, 1982), Taki Kōji described photography as "an organized act" rather than an isolated "production of images." The object of photographic research has never been the image alone, but also the act that produces that image and its material conditions. The philosopher Nishimura Kiyokazu (1997) likewise argued that the act of shooting is not merely the excision of an instant, but the result of all preceding events being condensed and arrested in the single moment of the shutter.
A number of scholars are currently engaged with this question of the photographic act—many of them with the sense that it is not completed in the instant the shutter is pressed. For example, in discussions surrounding Google Street View, photography is no longer the excision of a single image but has become a continuous act of capture generated across space, reconceived as an entity that ceaselessly congeals along a timeline. The recognition that "the shashin kōi is not completed in the instant of the shutter" can be said to be widely accepted.
On the basis of this perspective, I attempt to understand the shashin kōi as a "photography as experience (体験としての写真)" that encompasses the entire process of observation before shooting, the act of shooting, and appreciation after shooting.
This research takes this theory of the shashin kōi as its point of departure and connects it to the technical context of computational photography as it has developed since 2016.
Under the conditions of computational photography, many of the decisions that were previously designed as "photography" and as the "shashin kōi" can largely be replaced by mechanical configurations and algorithmic updates. Shutter pre-capture / pre-recording, Live Photo, subject tracking, AI-triggered shutter release, multi-frame synthesis, semantic segmentation, post-capture focus adjustment—in all of these, the decision is not issued at the moment of shooting itself, but rather in the two phases that precede it (prediction) and follow it (synthesis). Since composition, focus, and timing are all outcomes computed by the machine, the photographer, in the "decisive moment," has in fact decided nothing.
The subjectivity of photography therefore does not dwell in the instant of the shutter, but is distributed across the prior choice of "which parameters to cede to the algorithm" and the subsequent judgment of "which frame to select and how to modify it."
Correspondingly, the basis that underwrites the photographic image also shifts, gradually and by degrees, from the side of "indexicality (the record of 'having-been-there')" toward the side of "judgmentality (the perceptual judgment exercised in generation and selection)."

My Research Directions
At present my interests converge on:
1. The media archaeology of photography, photographic theory, and media theory—situating photography back within its technical history and visual-cultural lineage.
2. The ontology of photography and the problem of indexicality.
3. The intersection of the photographic / shashin tradition with contemporary installation art, especially expanded photography and installation-based modes of presentation.
4. Technical images, predictive media, and computational photography—that is, what happens to photography's indexicality and ontological status as images come increasingly to be generated and pre-empted by algorithms.

My Current Technical Competencies
* Photography and darkroom / imaging processes
* Installation and spatial presentation
* Digital / computational imaging tools

My Concerns
1. At present, my research merely describes a phenomenon—the shift of the photographic act from record to judgment. Doctoral research, however, is required to make a testable, original contribution to knowledge and to argue it through a systematic method. What I currently lack is a central line of argument that can stand on its own.
2. My research so far leans toward theoretical analysis; my works, too, are presentations in the register of photography and installation, with no engineered component—there is as yet no genuinely fabricated, actually functioning practical artifact.
